residual Hessian appears in the outer Witten square.

## Why the admissible stratum is indispensable

The radial no-go theorem proves that minimizing `1/2||Q^2||^2` over the full odd
charge space admits no nonzero critical curvature if the direction `Q` itself
is allowed. Persistent curvature therefore depends on fixed symbol, topology,
boundary data, normalization, or another genuine admissible constraint.

This also protects the construction from an important equivalence problem.
Curved infinity algebras can simplify drastically under unrestricted gauge
equivalence. Lazarev and Schedler classify a broad formal cyclic setting in
which nontrivially curved structures reduce sharply up to gauge equivalence.
That is not a direct no-go for a sheaf-valued, filtered, equivariant analytic
source, but it warns that MTT cannot quotient by every formal twist and still
expect physical sector information to survive.

The source category must therefore specify which morphisms preserve:

- principal symbol and operator domain;
- admissible strata and topological sector;
- shared-line holonomy;
- cyclic metric/trace normalization;
- gerbe and projective descent;
- orientation/branch typing; and
- the selected finite projection.

## Existing mathematics versus prospective MTT content

Established mathematics already supplies:

- superconnection curvature and Bianchi identities;
- cohesive dg enhancements of derived categories;
- curved dg and curved A-infinity transfer under stated hypotheses;
- Witten deformation and Morse localization;
- Jordan-Lie splitting of associative multiplication;
- Hodge heat semigroups;
- BV perturbative gauge reduction; and
- reversible-dissipative evolution frameworks.

The prospective MTT theorem is not any one item in that list. It would assert
that one selected nested object, with one shared differential line and one
admissible descent structure, emits all accepted lower shadows with their
connections, products, Hessians, holonomies, finite operators, and
normalizations.

## Corpus alignment

The candidate reuses rather than reopens current results:

- the q79 cohesive benchmark supplies an exact inner Maurer-Cartan residual
  `F(a)=d a+a^2` and its exact-zero Hodge tangent at its declared tier;
- local closure-repair descent already proves nonlinear defect-cost and
  gradient-flow descent under isometric overlaps;
- shared-circle work already separates internal phase from Lorentzian time;
- the finite q79 operational construction remains authoritative on its domain;
- current action work already proves that closure cost is not automatically the
  physical Lorentzian action.

The new work adds:

- the nonzero-defect residual Hessian;
- the central/noncentral curvature trichotomy;
- Jordan versus Lie readouts of the same curvature;
- the admissible-tangent radial no-go;
- intrinsic versus ambient Hessian separation;
- exact center-based scalar identifiability and compression; and
- the nested outer-Witten route at nonzero defect.

## Alternative route retained

A pure curved-superconnection route remains viable if the physical operator
must itself satisfy `d_Q^2=ad_Phi` off shell. A pure Witten route remains viable
for repair on a scalar-cost configuration space. The current conclusion is not
that one is wrong, but that they live at different levels and should be nested.

